'''VRChat Event Hosting''' is the process of planning, organizing, and running an event within [[VRChat]]. Events can be small informal gatherings or larger organized activities involving multiple participants.

==Planning an event==

Before hosting an event, organizers should determine what the event will involve and what is needed to run it.

===Choose the event type===

Determine the purpose and activities of the event.

Examples include:

*Community meetups
*Game sessions
*Roleplay
*World tours
*Avatar showcases
*Performances
*Social gatherings
*Competitions
*Educational or informational events

The type of event will determine what world, instance type, staff, and preparation may be required.

===Choose a world===

Select a VRChat world that is suitable for the event.

Consider:

*Maximum player capacity
*Supported platforms
*World performance
*Available features and interactions
*Instance compatibility
*The layout and size of the world
*Whether the world supports the planned activities

Organizers may want to visit and test the world before the event begins.

===Choose an instance type===

Choose an instance type based on how participants should be able to join.

Depending on the event, organizers may use public, friends, invite, invite+, or [[https://wiki.vrchat.com/wiki/Groups|group]] instances.

The appropriate instance type depends on the event's size, audience, and moderation requirements.

===Organize event staff===

Larger events may benefit from having multiple people responsible for different tasks.

Possible responsibilities include:

The [[Groups|Default Roles of your Group]] 

or any [https://wiki.vrchat.com/wiki/Groups#Creating_roles|other Role you want to create] for your Staff or members

A small event may only require one person to handle several of these responsibilities.

==Preparing the event==

Before the event begins, organizers should prepare the information and resources participants will need.

Useful information can include:

*Event name
*Date and time
*Starting world
*Instance type
*Event description
*Rules or guidelines
*Expected duration
*Platform requirements
*Accessibility information
*Community or group information

Event information can be shared through the organizer's preferred communication channels.

===Testing the event===

For larger or technically complex events, organizers may want to test the event before opening it to participants.

Testing can include:

*Checking the world and instance
*Testing important world interactions
*Checking audio and video features
*Testing event equipment
*Checking participant capacity
*Testing staff permissions and responsibilities
*Confirming that the planned activities work as expected

==Starting the event==

Before participants arrive, the organizer should enter the selected world and prepare the event area.

A basic startup procedure can include:

#Verify that the correct world is being used.
#Create or join the intended instance.
#Confirm that required staff are present.
#Check important world features.
#Prepare any equipment or activities.
#Welcome participants as they arrive.
#Explain any important rules or instructions.
#Begin the scheduled activities.

==During the event==

The host should keep the event organized and communicate important information to participants.

Depending on the event, the host may:

*Explain activities.
*Announce schedule changes.
*Answer participant questions.
*Direct participants to different areas.
*Coordinate event staff.
*Monitor the event for technical problems.

Staff should communicate with each other when assistance is required.

===Moderation===

Event organizers should follow the applicable VRChat rules as well as any rules established by the event or community.

Moderation should be handled consistently and appropriately for the situation.

Serious incidents can be documented and handled according to the organizer's normal procedures.

==Ending the event==

When the planned activities are finished, the host can announce that the event is ending.

The host may:

*Thank participants for attending.
*Provide information about future events.
*Share relevant community information.
*Collect feedback.
*Close or leave the event instance when appropriate.

==After the event==

After an event, organizers can review how it went and identify areas for improvement.

Useful information to review can include:

*Number of participants
*Event duration
*Technical problems
*Moderation incidents
*Participant feedback
*Activities that worked well
*Problems that occurred
*Changes to make for future events

Keeping notes from previous events can make future events easier to organize.

